  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Light rain continues into tonight. Some drought\nrelief is occurring, though flooding is not expected. Fog\ntonight as well. Showery conditions persist Friday into \nSaturday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nAs of early Thursday afternoon, the heavier rain has mostly \nmoved off to the east, but we still have intermittent light rain\nand drizzle for most places, especially closer to the New \nBrunswick border. Not much change is expected through tonight, \nwith some additional light rain (less than one quarter inch for \nmost places tonight). Another concern tonight with winds \ndecreasing and lots of lingering low level moisture is fog. \nCan't rule out some dense fog, but not enough confidence to put \ndense fog in the forecast. Probability of fog is about the same \nfor north vs south. \n\nDuring the day Friday, showers should increase to scattered to\nnumerous by midday as we will be under unstable NW flow on the\nwest side of low pressure in the Canadian Maritimes. Can't rule\nout a bit of thunder mainly in western portions of the area, but\nnot expecting anything strong or severe. Still abundant\ncloud cover during the day Friday, but clouds should lift some\nwith any fog dissipating by midday.\n\nThe surface/upper lows vertically stack across the Maritimes \nFriday night into Saturday, slowing the movement of the system. \nA disturbance moving around the Maritimes system will rotate \nacross the region Saturday. Shower chances will persist Friday \nnight into Saturday. The system then exits Saturday night. The \nentire forecast area should receive a soaking rain today through\ntonight. Generally expect rainfall totals of a half to three \nquarters of an inch across much of the forecast area today into \nSaturday, with areas of up to around an inch.",
